{templateType Trilobit\Core\Presentation\Styleguide\OverviewDefaultTemplate}

{*
 * What stays in view while the page scrolls, and the layer it is drawn in.
 *
 * There is nothing to put on a stage here: the specimen is the chrome of this
 * very page, which the theme switch above holds or lets go. What the page
 * shows is the tokens deciding it, as the themes name them - the list is
 * OverviewPresenter::CHROME_TOKENS, and every layer a theme declares has to be
 * named in it (tests/Template/StyleguideFoundationsTest).
 *}

{import '../../../components/table.latte'}

{block sgPage}
    <p class="l-measure">
        While the page scrolls, a theme may hold its banner and its navigation in view, each on a
        switch of its own. Ledger holds both wherever the window is wide enough for its side column.
        Atrium holds both too, the navigation under the banner, and once the content has scrolled up
        under them it makes them smaller: a lower banner, and the entries of the navigation at three
        quarters of their size with less room around them - the menu stays within reach and the
        content gets most of the window back. Scroll this page to see it; back at the top, both are
        drawn at their full size again. Nothing is held or made smaller on a window too narrow to give
        the room away, and nothing on paper.
    </p>

    <p class="l-measure">
        The smaller bands are the same tokens given other values, not a second description of them, and
        they change size over a moment rather than at once - except for somebody who asked for reduced
        motion, who gets the same two sizes with nothing drawn in between.
    </p>

    <p class="l-measure">
        Whatever is held is drawn in a layer above the content that scrolls under it. The menu of
        whoever is signed in is drawn above every layer named here, by the browser, and needs none of
        its own. The entries a navigation opens as a block over the page cannot be drawn there - a
        theme that unfolds them in place keeps them in the page - so they have a layer of their own,
        above what is held. A jump to a heading, and a link reached with the keyboard, stop under what
        is held rather than behind it.
    </p>

    {embed block table, caption: 'The tokens that decide what stays in view', testId: 'chrome-tokens'}
        {block tableHead}
            <tr>
                <th scope="col">Token</th>
                <th scope="col">What it decides</th>
            </tr>
        {/block}

        {block tableBody}
            <tr n:foreach="$chromeTokens as $token => $decides" data-testid="chrome-token-{$token}">
                <th scope="row"><code>{$token}</code></th>
                <td>{$decides}</td>
            </tr>
        {/block}
    {/embed}
{/block}
